# The transitive closure of F: every layer reachable from a composition through parts, at any
# depth, with the route that reached it. One row per (root, reached layer, path), so a layer
# reachable two ways appears twice, on purpose, because the duplication is the finding.
#
# The second level escapes every validator
#   Compositions nest, and the second level is where the model stops being checkable by any
#   validator. A holding composes a group, which composes its members. At one level "no part used
#   twice" is a key. At two it becomes "no leaf reachable by two paths", and the second path runs
#   through a document the first does not contain.
#
# No depth bound and no guess
#   SQL:2016's `CYCLE` clause stops the recursion exactly when a layer repeats within its own path,
#   which is the definition of the thing that would hang a reader, and `path` records the route
#   that closed the loop as data. A depth bound is a guess about how deep a legitimate composition goes;
#   this is the actual question.
#
# A diamond is not a cycle in the sense this walk stops on
#   One leaf reached down two different branches is not a repeat within a path, so it still comes
#   back twice, which is what the leaf rule needs. A depth-bounded walk truncates, and a walk
#   without the clause never returns on a document that closes a loop.
#   ⚠️ It IS a cycle in another sense, the undirected one `rank/graph_measures.sqlc` counts as
#   `m - n + c`, and there it is the double count itself. Both readings are about the same
#   diamond and neither is wrong: this walk must carry it, and `checks/jagged_layer` must accuse
#   it. Say which cycle is meant wherever the word is used.
#
# Which is four senses, not two, and they are four kinds of object
#   The instruction above is right and its count was short. The word carries four referents here,
#   and the first three are not readings of one thing, they are different kinds:
#     1. UNDIRECTED, a SUBSPACE of the edge space at dimension `m - n + c`. `rank/cycle_space.sqlc`
#        measures it; `checks/jagged_layer` holds the layer graph's at zero and
#        `checks/conversion_cycle_does_not_close` makes the unit graph's close.
#     2. DIRECTED, a PARTITION of the nodes into the classes of `F+ ∩ (F+)ᵀ`. That is
#        `checks/layers_move_together`, and it is also SQL's own word: the `CYCLE ... SET is_cycle`
#        clause below is the standard's, and it stops on this sense and not on the first.
#     3. A DUTY CYCLE, a RATIO, the fraction of a period a supply is live. It is a quantity rather
#        than a shape, it is normative in the schema, and it is the only one of the four that can
#        say the question does not apply: a stock has no cycle to be live in, so it takes
#        `notApplicable` rather than an omission.
#     4. THE LOOP YOU CAN WALK, a TRAVERSAL, which is what the other three compose to. The first
#        says an independent loop exists, the second says it can be traversed in the direction the
#        arrows point, the third supplies the period it returns after.
#
# Whether the fourth CLOSES is a question about the codomain and not about the graph
#   Walk the unit graph's one loop and you arrive back at the unit you started from, multiplied.
#   In the codomain of units it closed; in the codomain of unit-sizes it did not, and the quotient
#   between the two is what makes it look closed. So "does it close" is unanswerable until the
#   codomain is named, which is the same discipline every classification here owes.
#   ⭐ `checks/conversion_cycle_does_not_close` is where that is decided for the unit graph, and it
#   decides it without adjusting a filed number: it asks whether the failure to close is
#   distinguishable from zero given the width each factor was filed with.
#
# The product of the factors turns a recursion into a join
#   It carries the product of the factors along each path. A composed layer's remainder is defined
#   by recursion on `F`, and the natural form needs a sum in the recursive term, which SQL forbids
#   outright. A running product is a scalar per row and is allowed, so the closure can accumulate
#   `Π φ` on the way down and one `GROUP BY` at the end replaces the recursion entirely.
#
# `factor_absent` is a disjunction, not coalesced away
#   A part whose conversion nobody sized, or filed as an identity's output that nothing here
#   computes, poisons every path through it: the product is unknown rather than one, and a caller that reads the number without the flag has asserted the units
#   already agree.
#
# Where the product is actually consumed, which is not here
#   The relations composing this walk take its keys, its depth, its path and its cycle flag:
#   `composition/leaves.sqlc`, `composition/reachable.sqlc`, `composition/jagged_layers.sqlc`,
#   `rank/co_moving_layers.sqlc`, `rank/evaluation_order.sqlc` and `matrices.sql`'s tour. The
#   accumulation is read in `composition/remainder_frontier.sqlc`, the same walk carrying a stop
#   condition, and from there in `composition/settled_remainders.sqlc` and
#   `composition/carried_eliminations.sqlc`. So the two sections above argue for a capability this
#   relation offers a reader of the view rather than one its own consumers use, and the argument
#   is kept here because this is where the columns are.
#
# Do not merge it with the remainder frontier
#   `composition/remainder_frontier.sqlc` is this walk with a stop condition, and a reader who
#   finds the two nearly identical should read that file's header rather than merge them: a
#   remainder bottoms out earlier than a sum does, and stopping inside a recursion is not a filter
#   over this relation's result.
#
# The cycle clause's own path is not returned
#   `CYCLE ... USING route` keeps the path it walked as a column of a pseudo-type, which a view
#   cannot hold. The walk returns `is_cycle` and every column of its own, and not that one.
-- asrt:Fusion/asrt:Part followed transitively through pm.filing_identity.
WITH RECURSIVE
resolved AS (
    :compose(composition/parts.sqlc)
),
walk(root_filing, root_layer, filing, layer, depth, path,
     factor_low, factor_mode, factor_high, factor_absent) AS (
        SELECT p.composition, p.composed_layer, p.part_filing, p.part_layer, 1,
               ARRAY[p.composition  || '/' || p.composed_layer,
                     p.part_filing  || '/' || p.part_layer],
               coalesce(p.factor_low, 1), coalesce(p.factor_mode, 1), coalesce(p.factor_high, 1),
               p.factor_state IN ('absent', 'derivation')
        FROM resolved p
    UNION ALL
        SELECT w.root_filing, w.root_layer, p.part_filing, p.part_layer, w.depth + 1,
               w.path || (p.part_filing || '/' || p.part_layer),
               w.factor_low  * coalesce(p.factor_low,  1),
               w.factor_mode * coalesce(p.factor_mode, 1),
               w.factor_high * coalesce(p.factor_high, 1),
               w.factor_absent OR p.factor_state IN ('absent', 'derivation')
        FROM walk w
        JOIN resolved p ON p.composition = w.filing AND p.composed_layer = w.layer
) CYCLE filing, layer SET is_cycle USING route
SELECT root_filing, root_layer, filing, layer, depth, path,
       factor_low, factor_mode, factor_high, factor_absent, is_cycle
FROM walk
